  "problem": "Given that the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-4,$ what is the value of $a?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "At $x=-4,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{-}}({x}^{2}+3x) \\\\  & =(-4)^{2}+3(-4) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-5a.\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-4,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 4 & =-5a \\\\ a & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-4$ for $a=-\\frac{4}{5}.$",
